1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,909
Most of us met the determinant
as a formula: a d minus b c.

2
00:00:05,709 --> 00:00:09,933
It tells us whether a system of linear
equations has a unique solution.

3
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:16,859
It measures how much a linear transformation
scales area. Here, every area doubles.

4
00:00:18,259 --> 00:00:21,117
And when it's zero,
everything gets squashed flat.

5
00:00:21,917 --> 00:00:24,435
It even finds the area of any polygon.

6
00:00:26,835 --> 00:00:31,786
One of the most beautiful and unexpected
places I've found determinants is

7
00:00:31,786 --> 00:00:32,723
combinatorics.

8
00:00:33,523 --> 00:00:36,808
And today, I want to show you
how determinants can count.

9
00:00:38,608 --> 00:00:40,571
Let's start with a simple puzzle.

10
00:00:42,565 --> 00:00:43,930
Meet these two ants.

11
00:00:44,730 --> 00:00:46,778
They both live in a grid-like world.

12
00:00:47,578 --> 00:00:50,181
And they both want to
get back home, right now.

13
00:00:50,981 --> 00:00:56,311
On this grid there are rules. An ant can
only step up, or to the right. Never down,

14
00:00:56,311 --> 00:00:57,274
and never left.

15
00:00:58,074 --> 00:01:03,055
Of course, there are many paths home. The
blue ant could go this way, or this way.

16
00:01:03,055 --> 00:01:05,605
And the orange ant, this way, or that way.

17
00:01:06,405 --> 00:01:10,760
But today, they can't just take any
path. They had a fight, and if they meet,

18
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:12,400
they'll start fighting again.

19
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:18,098
So neither will set foot on any dot the
other one walks on. Not even later: ants

20
00:01:18,098 --> 00:01:19,322
leave a scent trail.

21
00:01:20,122 --> 00:01:23,685
So, how many pairs of paths get
them home without intersecting?

22
00:01:24,485 --> 00:01:29,451
You can always find one easily. Here,
the blue one goes up and around, and the

23
00:01:29,451 --> 00:01:32,634
orange one goes right and
around. They never meet.

24
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:35,120
And here's one where they cross.

25
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:40,983
Let's check them all, one by one. This
pair works. This one doesn't. Neither does

26
00:01:40,983 --> 00:01:43,045
this one. Or this one. And so on.

27
00:01:48,345 --> 00:01:50,798
Twenty pairs never meet, and sixteen do.

28
00:01:53,356 --> 00:01:58,232
I know what you're thinking: this is stupid.
There must be an easier way. Well, here

29
00:01:58,232 --> 00:01:59,393
comes an old friend.

30
00:02:00,193 --> 00:02:05,618
Consider this two-by-two matrix, A. Its
rows are the ants, and its columns are the

31
00:02:05,618 --> 00:02:06,081
houses.

32
00:02:06,881 --> 00:02:11,681
The first entry counts the paths from the
blue ant to its own home. There are six.

33
00:02:13,481 --> 00:02:18,127
The second entry is a little sneakier:
the paths from the blue ant to the orange

34
00:02:18,127 --> 00:02:19,753
ant's house. There are four.

35
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:26,874
And by symmetry, the orange ant gives the
same numbers the other way round: four,

36
00:02:26,874 --> 00:02:27,399
and six.

37
00:02:28,199 --> 00:02:32,807
Now find the determinant of A: six
times six, minus four times four,

38
00:02:33,607 --> 00:02:35,335
which is twenty. Voilà!

39
00:02:37,135 --> 00:02:38,756
The same number as before.

40
00:02:41,122 --> 00:02:44,023
In fact, there's nothing
special about this scenario.

41
00:02:44,823 --> 00:02:48,749
Make the grid bigger, and bring in a
third ant, with a house of its own.

42
00:02:49,549 --> 00:02:54,827
Build the same kind of matrix: three ants,
three houses, and each entry counts the

43
00:02:54,827 --> 00:02:56,887
paths from one ant to one house.

44
00:02:57,687 --> 00:02:59,394
Its determinant is fifty.

45
00:03:00,194 --> 00:03:05,409
And fifty is exactly the number of ways
all three ants can get home without any of

46
00:03:05,409 --> 00:03:06,999
their paths intersecting.

47
00:03:14,789 --> 00:03:16,304
So, why does this work?

48
00:03:17,104 --> 00:03:19,706
Let's get back to our two
ants, and work it out.

49
00:03:22,089 --> 00:03:24,265
Take a pair where the ants swap houses.

50
00:03:27,265 --> 00:03:31,446
The blue ant starts to the left of
the orange one, and ends to its right.

51
00:03:34,446 --> 00:03:36,473
So somewhere, they must share a dot.

52
00:03:37,873 --> 00:03:41,201
Take the first shared dot,
and swap the tails from there.

53
00:03:43,001 --> 00:03:46,222
Now each ant is at its own
home, and their paths meet.

54
00:03:51,789 --> 00:03:54,946
Now the other way: a pair
that meets on the way home.

55
00:03:56,546 --> 00:04:01,197
Swap the tails at the first shared dot,
and they end up in each other's houses.

56
00:04:02,997 --> 00:04:06,325
Swap again at the same dot, and
we're back where we started.

57
00:04:09,122 --> 00:04:12,429
So swapped pairs and meeting
pairs match up, one to one.

58
00:04:13,629 --> 00:04:15,997
This meeting pair goes
with this swapped pair.

59
00:04:18,397 --> 00:04:19,826
This one, with this one.

60
00:04:22,226 --> 00:04:23,847
And this one, with this one.

61
00:04:25,847 --> 00:04:26,829
And so on.

62
00:04:32,129 --> 00:04:33,302
Sixteen of each.

63
00:04:35,888 --> 00:04:37,147
Now, the formula.

64
00:04:38,547 --> 00:04:44,104
Six times six counts every pair going home:
twenty that never meet, and sixteen that

65
00:04:44,104 --> 00:04:44,435
meet.

66
00:04:46,235 --> 00:04:49,243
Four times four counts
the sixteen swapped pairs.

67
00:04:51,043 --> 00:04:53,283
They cancel the meeting pairs exactly.

68
00:04:54,883 --> 00:04:56,077
Leaving twenty.

69
00:04:57,877 --> 00:05:02,208
A bijective proof: the only kind
a combinatorialist fully trusts.

70
00:05:04,788 --> 00:05:09,552
You might be thinking: surely that's a
fluke of this little grid. Move the second

71
00:05:09,552 --> 00:05:13,492
house around, and each time, brute
force and the determinant agree.

72
00:05:14,292 --> 00:05:19,066
As long as the order stays the same: ant
one's house up and to the left of ant

73
00:05:19,066 --> 00:05:19,433
two's.

74
00:05:20,233 --> 00:05:25,584
Swap the order and there are no peaceful
routes at all. The determinant notices: it

75
00:05:25,584 --> 00:05:29,065
counts the swapped pairing
instead, with a minus sign.

76
00:05:31,055 --> 00:05:36,095
Here's the general statement, on our three
ants. Take a one-way network with some

77
00:05:36,095 --> 00:05:41,136
starts and some ends, and count the paths
from each start to each end. That's the

78
00:05:41,136 --> 00:05:41,572
matrix.

79
00:05:42,372 --> 00:05:47,872
Expand the determinant. Each term picks
an end for every start, a pairing, with a

80
00:05:47,872 --> 00:05:52,762
plus or a minus sign, and counts every
family of paths for that pairing.

81
00:05:53,562 --> 00:05:59,398
Here: two hundred and sixteen, minus
ninety-six, minus ninety-six, minus six, plus

82
00:05:59,398 --> 00:06:00,964
sixteen, plus sixteen.

83
00:06:01,764 --> 00:06:06,999
Of the two hundred and sixteen, only fifty
never touch. And when the starts and ends

84
00:06:06,999 --> 00:06:10,490
are lined up in order, a
crossed pairing always touches.

85
00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:16,919
Now the tail swap from before pairs every
touching family with one of opposite sign:

86
00:06:16,919 --> 00:06:22,482
one hundred ninety-eight against one hundred
ninety-eight. Mathematicians call this

87
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:25,498
a sign-reversing
involution. They all cancel.

88
00:06:26,298 --> 00:06:31,507
What's left is exactly the fifty families
that never touch. And that is true for any

89
00:06:31,507 --> 00:06:32,314
such network.

90
00:06:33,114 --> 00:06:40,269
This is the Lindström–Gessel–Viennot lemma.
Lindström found it in 1973; Gessel and

91
00:06:40,269 --> 00:06:45,679
Viennot rediscovered it in 1985
and showed how much it can do.

92
00:06:46,479 --> 00:06:51,594
Physicists call walkers like ours vicious
walkers. Given that our ants had a fight,

93
00:06:51,594 --> 00:06:52,580
that seems fair.

94
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:59,672
Brute force over n ants explodes. A
determinant takes about n cubed steps, or n to

95
00:06:59,672 --> 00:07:02,895
the omega with fast matrix multiplication.

96
00:07:03,695 --> 00:07:10,701
And omega keeps falling. Strassen broke
three in 1969, and last month it reached

97
00:07:10,701 --> 00:07:17,795
nine quarters. Proved by an AI model at
OpenAI, checked in Lean, and explained to

98
00:07:17,795 --> 00:07:22,788
the rest of us by Amir Shpilka.
Division of labour, 2026.

99
00:07:24,755 --> 00:07:30,243
So what can it count? The grid doesn't
matter. Any network of one-way roads with no

100
00:07:30,243 --> 00:07:33,352
loops: right, up, diagonal,
some roads missing.

101
00:07:34,152 --> 00:07:38,184
Count the routes from every start to
every end, and take the determinant.

102
00:07:38,984 --> 00:07:43,816
You get the number of ways for everyone
to travel without ever meeting. Here it's

103
00:07:43,816 --> 00:07:45,427
forty-two. Of course it is.

104
00:07:46,227 --> 00:07:51,347
So any puzzle that is secretly a family
of non-touching paths, a determinant can

105
00:07:51,347 --> 00:07:53,907
count. And these puzzles are everywhere.

106
00:07:55,888 --> 00:08:00,261
Here's a hexagon with every side of
length two, cut into little triangles.

107
00:08:01,061 --> 00:08:06,532
Glue two neighbouring triangles together
and you get a tile: a lozenge. Lozenges

108
00:08:06,532 --> 00:08:08,379
come in three orientations.

109
00:08:09,179 --> 00:08:14,781
A tiling covers the hexagon exactly with
lozenges. Nothing sticking out, nothing

110
00:08:14,781 --> 00:08:15,621
overlapping.

111
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:21,563
Here's one. Notice anything? It looks like
a stack of boxes in the corner of a room.

112
00:08:22,363 --> 00:08:26,864
It's completely flat. Your brain
is doing the 3D rendering for free.

113
00:08:27,664 --> 00:08:29,349
So how many tilings are there?

114
00:08:32,688 --> 00:08:35,120
Here's the trick. Fade out the flat tiles.

115
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:41,400
Every other tile has two vertical edges.
Enter through one, leave through the other,

116
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:44,923
and keep going. Two edges
on the left give two chains.

117
00:08:45,723 --> 00:08:51,089
Tiles never overlap, so the chains never
touch. And going backwards, any two chains

118
00:08:51,089 --> 00:08:54,128
that don't touch fill in
to exactly one tiling.

119
00:08:54,928 --> 00:09:00,385
Now squash the hexagon onto our square grid.
Each chain becomes a route of two steps

120
00:09:00,385 --> 00:09:04,933
right and two steps up, starting and
ending exactly where our ants do.

121
00:09:05,733 --> 00:09:11,041
So the number of tilings is twenty. The
same twenty. All this time, our ants were

122
00:09:11,041 --> 00:09:12,155
tiling a hexagon.

123
00:09:15,155 --> 00:09:20,954
Seen in 3D, a tiling is a stack of boxes
in a corner, each stack no taller than the

124
00:09:20,954 --> 00:09:22,003
ones behind it.

125
00:09:22,803 --> 00:09:27,779
A bigger box, three by three by three,
gives a bigger hexagon and three chains:

126
00:09:27,779 --> 00:09:29,480
three ants that never meet.

127
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:34,227
A three-by-three determinant counts
them: nine hundred and eighty.

128
00:09:35,027 --> 00:09:40,993
Percy MacMahon found a formula for every
box, over a century ago. Combinatorialists

129
00:09:40,993 --> 00:09:43,006
call these plane partitions.

130
00:09:44,988 --> 00:09:50,375
Another disguise. A mountain range: each
step goes up or down, and it never dips

131
00:09:50,375 --> 00:09:51,452
below sea level.

132
00:09:52,252 --> 00:09:56,860
How many ways can you draw two ranges,
one inside the other, without touching?

133
00:09:57,660 --> 00:10:03,061
A single range is counted by the Catalan
numbers. Put four of them in a two-by-two

134
00:10:03,061 --> 00:10:05,169
table: the determinant is three.

135
00:10:05,969 --> 00:10:08,188
And here they are. Exactly three.

136
00:10:10,155 --> 00:10:15,830
And one more, a beauty. This is an Aztec
diamond: rows of two, four, six squares,

137
00:10:15,830 --> 00:10:16,811
and back down.

138
00:10:17,611 --> 00:10:23,030
Tile it with dominoes. The smallest one
has two tilings. The next has eight. The

139
00:10:23,030 --> 00:10:24,182
next, sixty-four.

140
00:10:24,982 --> 00:10:26,198
Guess the next one.

141
00:10:28,798 --> 00:10:33,918
One thousand and twenty-four. Powers
of two, every time. That's suspicious.

142
00:10:35,888 --> 00:10:40,937
Colour the board like a chessboard, and
there are four kinds of domino. Give each

143
00:10:40,937 --> 00:10:44,677
kind a job: a flat step, a step
up, a step down, or nothing.

144
00:10:45,477 --> 00:10:51,138
Now the dominoes link up into paths, from
the left side to the right. Dominoes never

145
00:10:51,138 --> 00:10:56,461
overlap, so the paths never touch, and
every such family comes from exactly one

146
00:10:56,461 --> 00:10:56,933
tiling.

147
00:10:57,733 --> 00:11:03,128
So once again: count routes, take the
determinant. Twelve times two minus four times

148
00:11:03,128 --> 00:11:03,899
four. Eight.

149
00:11:04,699 --> 00:11:06,811
One size up: sixty-four.

150
00:11:07,611 --> 00:11:13,248
In general, order n gives two to the n
times n plus one over two. A determinant,

151
00:11:13,248 --> 00:11:15,291
hiding in a pile of dominoes.

152
00:11:17,288 --> 00:11:22,751
Now pick one tiling at random, every tiling
equally likely, on a big diamond, and

153
00:11:22,751 --> 00:11:23,560
let it grow.

154
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:25,811
This is order sixty.

155
00:11:26,611 --> 00:11:32,028
The corners freeze into brick walls. The
middle is chaos. And as the diamond grows,

156
00:11:32,028 --> 00:11:35,357
the boundary between them
becomes a perfect circle.

157
00:11:36,157 --> 00:11:38,227
It's called the arctic circle theorem.

158
00:11:41,221 --> 00:11:45,842
Back to the chessboard from the start.
Dominoes aren't paths, but there's a

159
00:11:45,842 --> 00:11:50,586
determinant trick here too. Shrink it
to a two-by-three board: three tilings.

160
00:11:51,386 --> 00:11:56,889
A domino joins a black square to a white
neighbour. Write a one wherever they touch.

161
00:11:56,889 --> 00:12:01,736
Each tiling picks one entry in every
row and every column: one term of the

162
00:12:01,736 --> 00:12:02,522
determinant.

163
00:12:03,322 --> 00:12:07,824
If determinants count pairings, this
should give three. It gives minus one.

164
00:12:08,624 --> 00:12:13,349
The terms come with signs: plus for
this one, minus for these two. Here the

165
00:12:13,349 --> 00:12:15,365
cancellation eats the good ones.

166
00:12:17,355 --> 00:12:23,912
In 1961 the physicist Pieter Kasteleyn
had an idea: if the signs don't cancel the

167
00:12:23,912 --> 00:12:26,422
way you want, change the signs.

168
00:12:27,222 --> 00:12:32,899
Put a minus sign on this one edge. Only the
all-vertical tiling uses it, so only its

169
00:12:32,899 --> 00:12:33,643
sign flips.

170
00:12:34,443 --> 00:12:40,175
Now every tiling carries the same sign.
The determinant is minus three. Ignore the

171
00:12:40,175 --> 00:12:41,014
sign: three.

172
00:12:42,988 --> 00:12:48,158
On a four-by-four board, the plain
determinant says there are zero tilings. Very

173
00:12:48,158 --> 00:12:49,580
confident, very wrong.

174
00:12:50,380 --> 00:12:55,668
Put minus signs on the vertical edges of
every other column, and the determinant

175
00:12:55,668 --> 00:12:57,783
gives thirty-six. Exactly right.

176
00:12:59,755 --> 00:13:04,912
Now the full chessboard: twelve million,
nine hundred eighty-eight thousand, eight

177
00:13:04,912 --> 00:13:08,246
hundred sixteen. The five-month
count, in an instant.

178
00:13:09,046 --> 00:13:14,413
The rule behind it: every little loop of
four edges carries an odd number of minus

179
00:13:14,413 --> 00:13:14,806
signs.

180
00:13:15,606 --> 00:13:21,199
And it's not really about chessboards.
Squares are vertices, dominoes are edges, and

181
00:13:21,199 --> 00:13:23,264
a tiling is a perfect matching.

182
00:13:24,064 --> 00:13:29,664
Kasteleyn, Temperley and Fisher made this
work for any graph you can draw without

183
00:13:29,664 --> 00:13:35,473
crossings: the FKT algorithm counts perfect
matchings of planar graphs in polynomial

184
00:13:35,473 --> 00:13:35,819
time.

185
00:13:36,619 --> 00:13:41,957
When a physicist needs to count twelve
million tilings, they don't count. They take

186
00:13:41,957 --> 00:13:45,366
a determinant, and then let
the board go to infinity.

187
00:13:47,354 --> 00:13:53,225
Let's step back. Ants that never meet.
Tilings of a hexagon. Boxes in a corner.

188
00:13:53,225 --> 00:13:56,570
Mountain ranges. Aztec
diamonds. Chessboards.

189
00:13:57,370 --> 00:13:59,610
All counted by one determinant each.

190
00:14:01,587 --> 00:14:06,684
And it's the same trick every time. Count
everything, good and bad, each with a

191
00:14:06,684 --> 00:14:07,006
sign.

192
00:14:07,806 --> 00:14:12,990
The bad ones pair up with opposite signs,
and cancel. What's left is exactly what

193
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:13,694
you wanted.

194
00:14:14,494 --> 00:14:20,847
For a computer scientist, that's remarkable.
Counting is usually hopeless: counting

195
00:14:20,847 --> 00:14:27,123
perfect matchings of a general graph is
sharp-P complete. That's Valiant, in 1979.

196
00:14:27,923 --> 00:14:33,252
But if your puzzle turns into non-touching
paths, or a matching on a planar graph,

197
00:14:33,252 --> 00:14:36,371
it becomes one determinant,
and polynomial time.

198
00:14:37,171 --> 00:14:40,691
Determinants can count,
because minus signs can cancel.

199
00:14:44,187 --> 00:14:49,563
As for our ants: they can finally go home
in peace. Twenty ways home, and they never

200
00:14:49,563 --> 00:14:50,331
had to meet.

201
00:14:52,631 --> 00:14:53,954
Thanks for watching.

